The indices are always in order, every index is always included and all the values of one array are always consecutive, so this fix should work unless the previously existing array is larger than the new array, in which case the tail end will remain whatever it was before.

TBH the handling on Node array in this methods seems like a really weird and unnecessary hack (these `/indices/` string are not serialized anywhere, they are generated a bit further up in the same method) so I would probably just simplify that whole mess a bit, for example like:

